"q": "You tune 3 hyperparameters with a 4 × 4 × 4 grid (64 runs). Later analysis shows only the learning rate mattered. How many distinct learning rates did you actually try, and what would 64 random trials have tried?",
"o": [
"64 and 64",
"4 with the grid; 64 with random search",
"16 with the grid; 4 with random search"
],
"k": 1,
"e": "A grid's points share coordinates: projected onto the learning-rate axis, 64 points collapse onto just 4 values. Random points have 64 distinct values on every axis, so the one that matters gets 64 tries. That's Bergstra and Bengio's argument."
